# The comment-drift state machine, locally and without a model.
#
# It is docs/working/comment-drift.tony's first half — commit-landed, gather, review — with one
# thing replaced: the review spawns a BROKERED task instead of an llm one, so the drive script
# is the worker. `verse task do complete <id> '<answer>'` supplies the findings, which means the
# answer is a fixture and the TIMING is the script's: a review lands exactly when the test says
# it does.
#
# That is the whole point. The defects this exists to reproduce are about ORDER, not about
# review quality:
#
#   - a review that finishes AFTER another commit landed wrote `reviewed: done` over the pending
#     state the newer one had just left, and that commit was never reviewed;
#   - a push of several commits only ever reviewed the tip, so a fix in any other commit of it
#     was never seen and its finding stayed open forever.
#
# Both are fixed here and both are still asserted: a record per commit addressed by its sha, and
# a chain that starts at the COMMIT rather than at the tip.
#
# Neither is reachable in under ten minutes against staging, and both are reachable in seconds
# here. The rules below are otherwise VERBATIM from the real charter, so a fix proved here is a
# fix that transfers — when they diverge, this file is the one that is wrong.
at: verse-dev.drift
reads: [ !verse-path [git] ]
---
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: git

- name: drift
  level: 10
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, commit]

- name: ledger
  level: 20
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, finding]

# A stand-in for the merge half's `train`, so the READ the real charter needs can be proved
# here: a train is above the head it describes, which makes reading the report a read DOWNWARD.
- name: train
  level: 30
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, train]
---
# 1. Each commit that has landed on main, as a fact this charter owns.
#
# THE SUBJECT IS THE COMMIT, not the tip, and that is the whole of the skipping fix. The tip
# names ONE commit per push, so a push of three reviewed one of them and the other two never
# became subjects of anything. The git source already reflects main's last N commits as
# `git:commit:<repo>/<sha>` — `git log -n <depth>` on the mirror — so the material was there
# and nothing read it.
#
# A RECORD PER COMMIT, addressed by the sha alone (`id:`, not `key:`), so every commit keeps its
# own `reviewed` state, its own subject for rule 3, its own task id — and an address anything
# downstream can spell. One slot was the cause of both defects this harness holds still: a late
# review wrote `done` over the state a newer commit had just left, and a push overwrote the
# previous commit's record before anything could review it. Neither has anywhere to land now.
#
# `git:head:verse` keeps only the job it is actually about: cutting the train (rule 4 in the
# real charter). It is out of the review half entirely.
- name: commit-landed
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [git, commit, "*"]
      value:
        repo: verse
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [verse-dev, drift, commit]
      id: $subject.value.sha
      value:
        repo: $subject.value.repo
        sha: $subject.value.sha
---
# 2. Before the review, the ledger is read onto the subject.
#
# THE ABSENCE OF `reviewed` IS THE START STATE, rather than a `pending` rule 1 writes, and that
# is what makes a re-install inert. A derive is a tony PATCH merged at depth, not a replace — so
# it overwrites exactly the fields it NAMES. Rule 1 naming `reviewed: pending` meant every
# install of this charter reset every record's flow state and re-armed every review that had
# already run: 131 llm tasks for a one-word charter edit, and invisible before the record was
# per commit because at width one it was a single extra review.
#
# So the derive names only what it owns — the facts about the commit — and `reviewed` belongs to
# the rules that move it. Nothing writes the start state; the start state is that nobody has
# written one.
- name: gather
  stratum: drift
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, commit, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path reviewed
  action:
    patch:
      reviewed: gathered
      outstanding: !list-paths
        at: [verse-dev, drift, finding, "*"]
        value:
          status: open
---
# 3. …and the commit is reviewed, once, against what is already known.
#
# The spawn is brokered rather than llm: no runner, no environment, no prompt worth reading —
# the drive script claims the task and completes it with the findings the test wants. Everything
# after the spawn (the hooks, the derive, the latch) is the real charter's.
- name: comment-drift
  stratum: drift
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, commit, "*"]
      value:
        reviewed: gathered
  action:
    spawn:
      vector: auto
      prompt: |
        Review $sha. The drive script answers this task; nothing here reads the prompt.
    onDone:
      derive:
      - path: [verse-dev, drift, finding]
        value: !each
          over: $data.findings
          key: $item.symbol
          value:
            symbol: $item.symbol
            status: $item.status
            says: $item.says
            at: $item.at
      patch:
        err: !delete null
        result: $data
        reviewed: done
        reviewedSha: $data.at
    onFailed:
      patch:
        err: $err
        result: !delete null
        reviewed: failed
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        err: !delete null
        result: !delete null
        reviewed: cancelled
---
# 4. A train, carrying the report of the commit it is about.
#
# THE READ THE REAL CHARTER NEEDS, proved here: the report of ONE commit, at an address the
# reader spells out of a sha it already has. `id:` on rule 1 is what makes that address exist —
# see the note on the patch below.
#
# The train is cut by the DRIVE SCRIPT rather than by a rule, which is the one place this file
# models the real charter loosely and does it deliberately. There, a train is cut on the tip and
# loaded later, when a proposal appears; the gap between the two is where the report lands. Here
# the script cuts it, so the harness says exactly when — including, in one case, before the
# review has landed at all.
- name: train-says
  stratum: train
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, train, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path says
  action:
    patch:
      # ONE ENTITY, NAMED. `id: $subject.value.sha` on rule 1 (rather than `key:`) makes the
      # record's address the place plus the sha — `verse-dev.drift.commit.aaaa1111` — so a reader
      # SPELLS it. With `key:` the engine composes (rule, subject, key) into
      # `head-moved@git:head:verse@aaaa1111`, which no charter can write down, and every reader
      # then has to describe the record instead: a `"*"` and a predicate on a field it happens
      # to know, with the cardinality checked at fire time. That read worked and it read badly,
      # and the badness was the address, not the operator.
      says: !get-path
        at: [verse-dev, drift, commit, $subject.value.sha]
        path: result.report
